{"folioDeck":1,"exportedAt":"2026-11-16T00:00:00.000Z","meta":{"author":"","language":"en","glossMode":"site","version":1,"createdAt":1794787200000,"updatedAt":1794787200000,"forkedFrom":null,"id":"gersounds","title":"German — Sounds and spelling","subtitle":"32 letters, umlauts, endings and stress rules — start here, beside Goethe A1","desc":"How German is read, before or beside the first word: 32 notes, one card each — 10 vowel spellings (ä, ö, ü, ei, ie, eu and äu, au, and the unstressed endings -e, -er and -en), 17 consonant letters and groups of letters whose sound is not the English one (ch, chs, sch, sp and st at the start of a word, s, ß, z, w, v, j, a final b, d or g, -ig, r, h, ng, pf, tsch), and 5 rules: the umlaut, how the spelling shows a long or a short vowel, the capital letter, where the stress falls, and the stress of a verb with a prefix. The front shows the letters. The back gives, for each position the letters can stand in, the sound in the International Phonetic Alphabet and the English sound it resembles, then up to three words of the Goethe A1 deck with a speaker beside each, the letters picked out and the word's own transcription: German spelling does not always decide the sound (ä is long in spät and short in März, v is f in von and f or v in November), so the words are chosen and each carries its transcription. Say the word, then press the speaker and compare. Of Goethe A1's 759 one-word headwords, 434 contain one of these spellings and 88 end in a b, d or g that is read p, t or k. THE ORDER is the vowels, the consonants, then the rules. At the default of ten new cards a day the deck takes 4 days; it is meant to be studied beside Goethe A1, not finished first. — WHAT IS NOT HERE: no description is written for this deck. Each English comparison is a cell of the first page named below, with that page's own labels (DE, AT and CH for Germany, Austria and Switzerland; RP and Scottish for the English accent meant); each context is a cell and each rule a sentence of the second (the two stress rules are from the third), with a cut marked […]. The letters that sound as an English speaker expects (f, k, l, m, n, p, t) have no card, and neither have the plain vowels a, e, i, o, u, whose length the spelling does not always show. No source here marks the stressed syllable of every word, so the two stress cards give the rule and transcribed words, and where a word's stress matters its transcription shows it (ˈ before the stressed syllable). The speaker is your device's German voice reading the example word, so a device without one is silent. — English comparisons: Wikipedia, “Help:IPA/Standard German”, revision 1376653424 of 2026-09-25. Contexts and rules: Wikipedia, “German orthography”, revision 1381205306 of 2026-10-20; Wikipedia, “Standard German phonology”, revision 1381175677 of 2026-10-20 (all CC BY-SA 4.0). Transcriptions: English Wiktionary, via kaikki.org (CC BY-SA 4.0). Example words and meanings: the Goethe A1 deck on this shelf (English Wiktionary via kaikki.org, CC BY-SA 4.0).","tags":["german","pronunciation","spelling","alphabet"],"types":{"desound":{"id":"desound","name":"German sounds and spelling","speechLang":"de-DE","fields":["Letters","Kind","Name","Rows","Says","Pairs","Examples"],"cards":[{"name":"Spelling → sound","front":"<div class=\"uc-ask\">{{Kind}}</div><div class=\"uc-snd\">{{Letters}}</div>","back":"{{FrontSide}}{{#Name}}<div class=\"uc-name\">{{Name}}</div>{{/Name}}<hr>{{#Rows}}{{Rows}}{{/Rows}}{{#Says}}<div class=\"uc-field\">{{Says}}</div>{{/Says}}{{#Pairs}}{{Pairs}}{{/Pairs}}{{#Examples}}<div class=\"uc-rows uc-alone\">{{Examples}}</div>{{/Examples}}"}],"css":".card {\n  text-align: center;\n  font-size: 17px;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n.uc-ask {\n  margin-bottom: 14px;\n  font-size: 11px;\n  letter-spacing: 0.14em;\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  opacity: 0.55;\n}\n.uc-snd {\n  font-size: 54px;\n  line-height: 1.15;\n}\n.uc-name {\n  margin-top: 4px;\n  font-size: 13px;\n  letter