"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"jung\"></span>ju<b>ng</b></span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/jʊŋ(k)/</span>young</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"die Zeitung\"></span>die Zeitu<b>ng</b></span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈt͡saɪ̯tʊŋ/</span>newspaper</span></div></div></div>","Says":"","Pairs":"","Examples":""}},{"id":"u_gersounds_26","num":"26","key":"letter:pf","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"pf","Kind":"German letters","Name":"","Rows":"<div class=\"uc-sr\"><div class=\"uc-ipa\">[pf]</div><div class=\"uc-like\">cu<b>pf</b>ul</div><div class=\"uc-rows\"><span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"der Kopf\"></span>der Ko<b>pf</b></span><span class=\"uc-rg\">head</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"das Pfund\"></span>das <b>Pf</b>und</span><span class=\"uc-rg\">a metric pound</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"der Apfel\"></span>der A<b>pf</b>el</span><span class=\"uc-rg\">apple</span></div></div></div>","Says":"","Pairs":"","Examples":""}},{"id":"u_gersounds_27","num":"27","key":"letter:tsch","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"tsch","Kind":"German letters","Name":"","Rows":"<div class=\"uc-sr\"><div class=\"uc-ipa\">[tʃ]</div><div class=\"uc-like\">ma<b>tch</b></div><div class=\"uc-rows\"><span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"tschüss\"></span><b>tsch</b>üss</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/t͡ʃʏs/</span>bye</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"deutsch\"></span>deu<b>tsch</b></span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/dɔʏ̯t͡ʃ/</span>German</span></div></div></div>","Says":"","Pairs":"","Examples":""}},{"id":"u_gersounds_28","num":"28","key":"rule:umlaut","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"ä ö ü","Kind":"German spelling","Name":"the umlaut","Rows":"","Says":"The accented letters ⟨ä, ö, ü⟩ are used to indicate the presence of umlauts (fronting of back vowels). […] When it is not possible to use the umlauts (for example, when using a restricted character set) the characters ⟨Ä, Ö, Ü, ä, ö, ü⟩ should be transcribed as ⟨Ae, Oe, Ue, ae, oe, ue⟩ respectively […]; simply using the base vowel (e.g., ⟨u⟩ instead of ⟨ü⟩) would be wrong and misleading.","Pairs":"","Examples":"<span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"schon\"></span>schon</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ʃoːn/</span>already</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"schön\"></span>schön</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ʃøːn/</span>beautiful</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"für\"></span>für</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/fyːɐ̯/</span>for</span></div>"}},{"id":"u_gersounds_29","num":"29","key":"rule:length","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"nn  h","Kind":"German spelling","Name":"a long or a short vowel","Rows":"","Says":"A doubled consonant after a vowel indicates that the vowel is short, while a single consonant often indicates the vowel is long, e.g. Kamm ('comb') has a short vowel /kam/, while kam ('came') has a long vowel /kaːm/. […] A silent ⟨h⟩ indicates the vowel length in certain cases.","Pairs":"","Examples":"<span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"kommen\"></span>kommen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈkɔmən/</span>to come</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"der Mann\"></span>der Mann</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/man/</span>man</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"zehn\"></span>zehn</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/t͡seːn/</span>ten</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"gut\"></span>gut</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ɡuːt/</span>good</span></div>"}},{"id":"u_gersounds_30","num":"30","key":"rule:capital","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"A a","Kind":"German spelling","Name":"the capital letter","Rows":"","Says":"A typical feature of German spelling is the general capitalization of nouns and of most nominalized words. In addition, capital letters are used: at the beginning of sentences […]; in the formal pronoun Sie 'you' and the determiner Ihr 'your' […].","Pairs":"","Examples":"<span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"das Essen\"></span>das Essen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\">eating</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"essen\"></span>essen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈɛsən/</span>to eat</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"Sie\"></span>Sie</span><span class=\"uc-rg\">you (polite, singular and plural)</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"sie\"></span>sie</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ziː/</span>she</span></div>"}},{"id":"u_gersounds_31","num":"31","key":"rule:stress","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"ˈ","Kind":"German stress","Name":"where the stress falls","Rows":"","Says":"In German words there is always one syllable carrying main stress, with all other syllables either being unstressed or carrying a secondary stress. […] Traditionally, word stress is seen as falling onto the first stem syllable.","Pairs":"","Examples":"<span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"der Name\"></span>der Name</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈnaːmə/</span>name</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"immer\"></span>immer</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈɪmɐ/</span>at all times without exception</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"das Hotel\"></span>das Hotel</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/hoˈtɛl/</span>hotel</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"die Entschuldigung\"></span>die Entschuldigung</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ɛntˈʃʊldiɡʊŋ/</span>excuse me</span></div>"}},{"id":"u_gersounds_32","num":"32","key":"rule:stress-prefix","type":"desound","fields":{"Letters":"be-  ab-","Kind":"German stress","Name":"a verb with a prefix","Rows":"","Says":"Words beginning with be-, ge-, er-, ver-, zer-, ent-, emp- and a few other inseparable prefixes are stressed on the root. Words beginning with the separable prefixes ab-, auf-, ein-, vor-, and most prepositional adverbs are stressed on the prefix.","Pairs":"","Examples":"<span class=\"uc-lab\">as in</span><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"verstehen\"></span>verstehen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/fərˈʃteːən/</span>to hear and interpret</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"bezahlen\"></span>bezahlen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/bəˈtsaːlən/</span>to pay</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"bekommen\"></span>bekommen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/bəˈkɔmən/</span>to receive</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"aufstehen\"></span>aufstehen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈʔaufˌʃteːən/</span>to get up</span></div><div class=\"uc-row\"><span class=\"uc-rz\"><span class=\"uc-tts uc-exsay\" data-say=\"einkaufen\"></span>einkaufen</span><span class=\"uc-rg\"><span class=\"uc-ri\">/ˈaɪ̯nˌkaʊ̯fən/</span>to shop</span></div>"}}],"gloss":{}}